UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'adjacent_fact', $a16C$Invokes a real testicular hormone (testosterone) but assigns it as the reason for the external location$a16C$ FROM q;

-- Q17 [Male Reproductive Anatomy | 3B | easy | Skill 1 | ans A]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Reproduction', $sub17$Male Reproductive Anatomy$sub17$, $qt17$A histologist examines a stained cross-section of a human testis and locates a cluster of cells sitting in the connective-tissue spaces between two adjacent seminiferous tubules, outside the tubule walls. When a researcher wants to identify the population that is the principal source of testicular testosterone, the cells in this location are the ones to sample.$qt17$, $op17$[{"label": "A", "text": "The interstitial (Leydig) cells located between the seminiferous tubules secrete testosterone."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The Sertoli (sustentacular) cells surrounding the developing germ cells inside the tubules secrete testosterone."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The spermatogonia lining the basement membrane inside the tubule secrete testosterone."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The epithelial cells lining the epididymis secrete testosterone."}]$op17$::jsonb, 'A', $ex17$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Gametogenesis & Reproductive Anatomy (BIO)'. The answer is A because the testosterone-secreting cells of the testis are the Leydig cells, whose alternate name—interstitial cells—reflects their position in the spaces between the seminiferous tubules, exactly the location described in the stem. The anatomy text states that "Testosterone, an androgen, is a steroid hormone produced by Leydig cells. The alternate term for Leydig cells, interstitial cells, reflects their location between the seminiferous tubules in the testes." The cells inside the tubule walls (Sertoli cells and spermatogonia) instead support and form the developing sperm, and the epididymis is a downstream duct for sperm maturation rather than a site of steroid synthesis. (Choice A) Correct: the interstitial (Leydig) cells between the tubules are the source of testicular testosterone. (Choice B) Incorrect: Sertoli (sustentacular) cells surround the germ cells inside the tubules and "secrete signaling molecules that promote sperm production," not testosterone. (Choice C) Incorrect: spermatogonia are the diploid germ-line stem cells that divide to form sperm, not a hormone-secreting population. (Choice D) Incorrect: the epididymis is a coiled duct "where newly formed sperm continue to mature," not a steroid-producing tissue. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because it requires recalling the established identity and location of the testicular cell type that produces testosterone.$ex17$, 'easy', '3B', $cc17$Gametogenesis & Reproductive Anatomy (BIO)$cc17$, 'biology', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
