-- Q18 [Male Reproductive Anatomy | 3B | medium | Skill 2 | ans B]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Reproduction', $sub18$Male Reproductive Anatomy$sub18$, $qt18$A researcher collects sperm from a male and resuspends them in a buffered medium that is identical to seminal vesicle secretions in every respect except that it lacks fructose. The sperm themselves are structurally normal, with intact mid-piece mitochondria and an undamaged flagellum. Compared with sperm in complete seminal vesicle fluid, what does this experiment most likely predict?$qt18$, $op18$[{"label": "A", "text": "Sperm motility would be impaired, because seminal vesicle fluid normally provides the alkaline buffer that neutralizes the acidic female reproductive tract"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Sperm motility would be impaired, because the fructose normally supplied by the seminal vesicles fuels the mitochondrial ATP production that powers the flagellum"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Sperm production would fall, because seminal vesicle fructose is the energy source that drives spermatogenesis in the seminiferous tubules"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Sperm motility would be unaffected, because the mid-piece mitochondria generate ATP from their own internal glycogen stores independent of seminal fluid"}]$op18$::jsonb, 'B', $ex18$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Gametogenesis & Reproductive Anatomy (BIO)'. The answer is B because seminal vesicle fluid contains large amounts of fructose, and the sperm mitochondria use that fructose to generate the ATP that powers flagellar movement; remove the fructose and the energy supply for motility is lost even though the cell is structurally intact. The text states that "Seminal vesicle fluid contains large amounts of fructose, which is used by the sperm mitochondria to generate ATP to allow movement through the female reproductive tract," and separately that the "ATP produced by these mitochondria will power the flagellum... enabling it to move the entire sperm cell." Because the sperm in this experiment are normal but starved of their external sugar substrate, the mitochondria cannot make adequate ATP and motility is predicted to fall. (Choice A) Neutralizing the acidic tract is a real role of seminal/semen chemistry, but the text attributes the alkaline, milky fluid to the prostate gland, not to the seminal vesicle, so this misnames the gland responsible. (Choice B) is correct because it links the missing fructose directly to the mitochondrial ATP that drives the flagellum. (Choice C) wrongly relocates the fructose to spermatogenesis in the seminiferous tubules; fructose acts downstream on already-formed sperm to fuel movement, not upstream on sperm manufacture. (Choice D) is false because the text specifies that the mitochondria depend on externally supplied fructose for ATP, not on any internal glycogen reserve, so removing fructose does affect motility. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires predicting an experimental outcome by tracing the consequence of removing one defined input (fructose) through the mechanism that converts it to flagellar ATP.$ex18$, 'medium', '3B', $cc18$Gametogenesis & Reproductive Anatomy (BIO)$cc18$, 'biology', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
