-- Q23 [Female Reproductive Anatomy | 3B | medium | Skill 2 | ans C]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Reproduction', $sub23$Female Reproductive Anatomy$sub23$, $qt23$During the luteal phase, a clinician images the uterine wall of a patient and identifies the single endometrial layer that has thickened under progesterone to receive a potential blastocyst and that, in the absence of fertilization, will detach and be expelled as menstrual flow while the deeper endometrial tissue is preserved to regenerate the lining. Which layer has the clinician identified?$qt23$, $op23$[{"label": "A", "text": "The stratum basalis, because it is the endometrial layer that supplies the implantation site and is shed with each menstrual cycle"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The myometrium, because it is the layer that thickens to receive the embryo and is then sloughed off as menstrual blood"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The stratum functionalis, because it is the endometrial layer that thickens for implantation and is the only layer shed at menstruation"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The perimetrium, because it is the innermost lining that thickens for implantation and detaches during menses"}]$op23$::jsonb, 'C', $ex23$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Gametogenesis & Reproductive Anatomy (BIO)'. The answer is C because the stratum functionalis is the thicker endometrial layer that grows and thickens in response to estrogen and progesterone, provides the implantation site for the fertilized egg, and is the only endometrial layer shed at menstruation. The text states that the stratum functionalis "grows and thickens in response to increased levels of estrogen and progesterone" and that "this inner functional layer provides the proper site of implantation for the fertilized egg, and—should fertilization not occur—it is only the stratum functionalis layer of the endometrium that sheds during menstruation." The deeper stratum basalis is part of the lamina propria adjacent to the myometrium and is retained ("this layer does not shed during menses"), allowing it to regenerate the functionalis each cycle. The two named non-endometrial layers, the myometrium (smooth muscle) and perimetrium (serous covering), are not the implantation lining. (Choice A) The stratum basalis is incorrect because it is the layer that does NOT shed during menses; it is retained adjacent to the myometrium and serves as the regenerative base rather than the implantation/shedding layer. (Choice B) The myometrium is incorrect because it is the thick middle layer of smooth muscle responsible for uterine contractions, not the endometrial lining that thickens for implantation or sheds as menstrual flow. (Choice C) The stratum functionalis is correct because it is the endometrial layer that thickens under progesterone to support implantation and is the only layer shed at menstruation. (Choice D) The perimetrium is incorrect because it is the most superficial serous membrane covering the exterior of the uterus, not an inner lining that thickens or detaches during menses. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires interpreting a clinical scenario and reasoning from the described functions—thickening for implantation, selective shedding, and preservation of a regenerative base—to deduce which specific uterine layer is being identified.$ex23$, 'medium', '3B', $cc23$Gametogenesis & Reproductive Anatomy (BIO)$cc23$, 'biology', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
