-- Q24 [Female Reproductive Anatomy | 3B | hard | Skill 2 | ans D]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Reproduction', $sub24$Female Reproductive Anatomy$sub24$, $qt24$A clinician tracing the path of a single sperm cell in a successful natural conception wants to identify the exact site where it most often meets the still-moving oocyte, given that the oocyte is being swept from the fimbriae toward the uterus and the resulting zygote will continue on to implant. At which region of the uterine tube does this encounter most often take place?$qt24$, $op24$[{"label": "A", "text": "In the infundibulum, the distal funnel whose fimbriae first capture the ovulated oocyte"}, {"label": "B", "text": "In the isthmus, the narrow medial segment that joins the uterine tube to the uterus"}, {"label": "C", "text": "In the uterus, where the oocyte arrives at the endometrium before the encounter"}, {"label": "D", "text": "In the ampulla, the middle region of the uterine tube between the infundibulum and isthmus"}]$op24$::jsonb, 'D', $ex24$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Gametogenesis & Reproductive Anatomy (BIO)'. The answer is D because fertilization most often occurs in the ampulla, the middle region of the uterine tube, where sperm typically meet the egg while it is still moving toward the uterus. The text states that "the middle region of the tube, called the ampulla, is where fertilization often occurs" and that "when fertilization does occur, sperm typically meet the egg while it is still moving through the ampulla." Chaining the transport direction backward, the fimbriae of the infundibulum first capture the oocyte, cilia and muscular contractions then move it medially through the ampulla (where it meets sperm), and only afterward does the resulting zygote travel onward through the isthmus into the uterus to implant. The sperm must therefore traverse the uterus and isthmus to reach the oocyte still in the mid-tube ampulla. (Choice A) The infundibulum and its fimbriae are where the ovulated oocyte is first received, not where fertilization typically occurs, which is more distally in the ampulla. (Choice B) The isthmus is the narrow medial end connecting the tube to the uterus, a segment the zygote passes through after fertilization rather than the usual fertilization site. (Choice C) The uterus is the site of implantation, not fertilization; the oocyte is fertilized while still moving through the tube before reaching the endometrium. (Choice D) The ampulla is the middle region between the infundibulum and isthmus and is the region where the moving oocyte usually meets sperm. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires integrating uterine-tube regionalization with the directional transport of the oocyte to deduce the encounter site rather than recalling a single labeled fact.$ex24$, 'hard', '3B', $cc24$Gametogenesis & Reproductive Anatomy (BIO)$cc24$, 'biology', 2, 150
  ) RETURNING id
)
